.TH LEAFDUMP 1 "2026-09-20" "leafdump 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
leafdump \- make nested data greppable, one value per line
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B leafdump
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B leafdump
prints one line for every value in a nested data file, and each line is the
complete path to that value.  A service configuration, an API response, a
cloud CLI dump or a stream of log records becomes something
.BR grep (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR cut (1)
and
.BR awk (1)
already know how to read \(em including the case those handle worst: a
document that arrives as one enormous line with no newlines in it, where a
match is the whole file and a pager shows a wall of text.
.PP
The question it answers is the one that comes up in front of a machine that is
misbehaving: what is actually in this file, where does that setting live, and
which of these hosts disagrees with the others.  Answering it needs no query
language, no editor and no scripting \(em only the filters already to hand.
By default the path is written in a pseudo\-perl notation; fifteen others,
including valid Python, Go, jq and JSON Pointer, are selectable with
.BR \-\-template .
.PP
Two further modes share the same reader.  One converts between serialisation
formats \(em JSON, YAML, TOML, MessagePack, CBOR and the rest \(em so a file
in a format nothing local can open becomes one that everything can.  The other
merges several documents into a single structure, which is how a base
configuration and its per\-host overrides are compared or combined.  See the
.B Merging
options under
.B OPTIONS
below.
.PP
Support for each format depends on optional Python packages; formats whose
dependencies are absent are hidden from
.B \-\-help
and replaced by a one\-line notice describing how to enable them.  The program
itself, and every path notation in it, needs nothing but Python.  See
.B FORMATS
below and
.BR "leafdump \-\-list\-formats" .
.
.SH OUTPUT NOTATIONS
The path dump is rendered from a
.IR template :
a table of literal strings saying how one leaf is spelled as a line of some
language.  Select one with
.BR \-\-template ,
list them with
.BR \-\-list\-templates ,
and see
.B TEMPLATES
below for writing your own.
.
.SS perl (default)
The original notation.  Each line is the path to a leaf with the leaf value
appended:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ROOT.{definitions}.{vendor}.{type}."object"
ROOT.{presets}.0.{name}."default"
ROOT.{presets}.0.{hidden}.true
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mapping keys appear as
.BR .{ key },
sequence indices as
.BI . n\fR,
strings and numbers alike as
\fB."\fIvalue\fB"\fR,
and booleans bare as
.B .true
or
.BR .false .
Numbers being quoted like strings is the notation's one real loss: 1 and "1"
render identically.
.
.SS python
Every line is a valid Python assignment statement:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ROOT['definitions']['vendor']['type'] = 'object'
ROOT['presets'][0]['name'] = 'default'
ROOT['presets'][0]['hidden'] = True
ROOT['presets'][0]['tags'] = []
.EE
.RE
.PP
Values are rendered with Python's
.BR repr (),
so quoting and escaping are exact and unambiguous, numbers keep their type,
and empty containers are representable.  The output can be pasted into an
interpreter or replayed line by line to rebuild the structure.
.
.SS The rest
The same leaf,
.BR "{\(dqhosts\(dq: [{\(dqname\(dq: \(dqweb\-01\(dq}]}" ,
in each remaining built\-in notation:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 14
.B javascript
.B ROOT.hosts[0].name = \(dqweb\-01\(dq;
.TP
.B cpp
.B ROOT["hosts"][0]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq;
.TP
.B go
.B ROOT["hosts"][0]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B rust
.B ROOT["hosts"][0]["name"] = json!(\(dqweb\-01\(dq);
.TP
.B ruby
.B ROOT["hosts"][0]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B php
.B $ROOT["hosts"][0]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq;
.TP
.B lua
.B ROOT["hosts"][1]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B r
.B ROOT[["hosts"]][[1]][["name"]] <\- \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B jq
.B .["hosts"][0]["name"] = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B jsonpath
.B $.hosts[0].name = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B jsonpointer
.B /hosts/0/name = \(dqweb\-01\(dq
.TP
.B dotted
.B ROOT.hosts.0.name=web\-01
.TP
.B shell
.B ROOT[hosts.0.name]=\(aqweb\-01\(aq
.PD
.PP
.B lua
and
.B r
count array elements from 1, because those languages do.
.B dotted
quotes nothing, which makes it the easiest to hand to
.BR cut (1)
and
.BR awk (1)
and the easiest to confuse with a value containing a separator \(em pair it
with
.BR \-e .
.B shell
emits a
.B "declare \-A"
header and flattens the nesting into the key, so the output can be sourced
into bash.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-f ", " \-\-input\-format ", " \-\-from " \fIFMT\fR"
Format of the input.  Default
.BR auto ,
which detects from the file extension and then from the leading bytes.  Pass
an explicit format when reading binary data from a pipe.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-multipacket
Treat each line of the input as a separate document rather than reading the
whole stream as one.  Formats with native multi\-document support (YAML,
JSONL, MessagePack, CBOR, BSON, Avro) always split on their own record
boundaries regardless of this flag.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse
Process documents in reverse order, newest first \(em the usual want when the
input is an append\-only log.  Implies
.BR \-\-multipacket .
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-encoding " ENC"
Character encoding for text input formats.  Default
.BR utf\-8 .
.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-t ", " \-\-output\-format ", " \-\-to " \fIFMT\fR"
Format to emit.  Default
.BR perl .
Any pseudocode template name works here too
.RB ( python ", " javascript ", " go ", " r ", " ...),
as does any writable data format, which converts instead of flattening.
Giving both a data format and
.B \-\-template
is an error, since one of the two would have to be ignored.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.  Binary formats refuse to write to a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-indent " N"
Indentation width for structured text output.  Default 2.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Emit structured output on a single line with no indentation.  Overrides
.BR \-\-indent .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort\-keys
Sort mapping keys.  Python preserves insertion order, so output is already
deterministic; this makes it comparable across inputs whose keys were written
in different orders.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Escape all non\-ASCII characters in text output.
.TP
.BI \-\-null\-policy " POLICY"
What to do with null values when the target format cannot represent them
(TOML, NestedText).
.B keep
leaves them for the codec to handle,
.B drop
removes the key entirely, and
.B empty
writes an empty string.  Default
.BR keep .
.TP
.BI \-\-avro\-schema " FILE"
Read an Avro writer schema (JSON) from
.I FILE
instead of inferring one from the data.  Inference is approximate; supply a
schema for anything load\-bearing.
.
.SS Path dump
These affect the pseudocode notations only, not the data formats.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-template " \fINAME\fR|\fIFILE\fR"
Notation to render paths in: a built\-in name
.RB ( perl ,
.BR python ,
.BR javascript ,
.BR cpp ,
.BR go ,
.BR rust ,
.BR ruby ,
.BR php ,
.BR lua ,
.BR r ,
.BR jq ,
.BR jsonpath ,
.BR jsonpointer ,
.BR dotted ,
.BR shell ),
a name found on the template search path, or the path to a JSON or TOML
template file.  Default
.BR perl .
Every built\-in is also an output format of its own name, so
.B \-t go
and
.B \-T go
are the same request; only
.B \-T
can reach a template held in a file.  See
.B TEMPLATES
below.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-escape\-special
Escape carriage returns, line feeds and tabs in leaf values so every leaf
stays on one line.  Affects only the notations that quote minimally
.RB ( perl ,
.BR dotted ,
.BR shell );
the rest escape unconditionally, because their quoting styles have to.
.TP
.B \-\-perl\-compat
Restore the quirks of the original Perl script this tool grew out of (see
.BR HISTORY ),
reproducing its output byte for byte: null renders as
.RB \(lq "" \(rq
rather than
.BR undef ,
empty maps and arrays emit no line at all, and
.B \-e
escapes only CR and LF.  Only the
.B perl
notation claims that compatibility; other templates ignore this flag rather
than adopting a second language's bugs.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " NAME"
Name of the root node in dumped paths.  Default: whatever the template calls
it \(em
.B ROOT
for most,
.B $ROOT
for
.BR php ,
.B .
for
.BR jq .
.
.SS Merging
Multiple input files are merged into a single structure before output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-merge
Emit each input document separately instead of merging them into one
structure.
.TP
.BI \-\-merge\-strategy " STRATEGY"
How to combine mappings that define the same key.
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 10
.B deep
Recurse; later documents win at the leaves.  Default.
.TP
.B shallow
The later document replaces the whole value.
.TP
.B last
The later document wins outright.
.TP
.B first
The earlier document wins outright.
.TP
.B collect
Gather conflicting values into a deduplicated list.
.PD
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-list\-merge " STRATEGY"
How to combine arrays at the same path.
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 10
.B concat
Append.  Default.
.TP
.B union
Append, dropping structural duplicates.
.TP
.B replace
The later array wins.
.TP
.B keep
The earlier array wins.
.TP
.B index
Merge element\-wise by position.
.PD
.RE
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dedup
After merging, remove structurally identical elements from every array,
keeping the first occurrence.  Comparison is by value
.I and
type, so
.BR 1 ,
.B 1.0
and
.B true
remain distinct even though Python considers them equal.
.TP
.BI \-\-wrap\-key " MODE"
Instead of merging, key each document by its source:
.BR basename ,
.B stem
(basename without extension),
.BR path ,
or
.BR index .
Also useful to give a top\-level mapping to formats that require one (TOML,
BSON, Avro).  Default
.BR none .
.
.SS Information
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-list\-formats
List every known format with its availability, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-templates
List every pseudocode template \(em built\-in first, then any found on the
search path \(em with one rendered example of each, then exit.  With
.B \-\-porcelain
the fields are tab\-separated: name, source, aliases, example, summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-help\-template " [NAME]"
Explain one template field by field, then exit.  With no
.IR NAME ,
explain how to write one.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-template " NAME"
Print a template as JSON \(em the starting point for editing one into a
notation of your own \(em then exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-help\-format " FMT"
Explain one format in detail \(em dependencies, caveats and round\-trip
limitations \(em then exit.  Works for formats that are not installed.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Make
.B \-\-list\-formats
emit tab\-separated fields for scripts and shell completion instead of prose.
Fields are: name, usable directions, encoding kind, availability
.RB ( yes / no ),
comma\-separated aliases, summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-direction " DIR"
Restrict
.B \-\-list\-formats
to readable
.RB ( in )
or writable
.RB ( out )
formats.  Default
.BR any .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information \(em which formats are currently enabled, and which
pseudocode notations are available \(em and exit.
.
.SH TEMPLATES
A template is a JSON (or TOML) file of literal strings describing how one leaf
is spelled as a line of some language.  It is data: nothing in it is imported,
evaluated or executed, and the loader rejects any field, placeholder or
quoting style it does not recognise.
.PP
Start from the closest built\-in and edit it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
leafdump \-\-dump\-template javascript > kotlin.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
or state only the differences and inherit the rest with
.BR base :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
{
  "base": "javascript",
  "name": "kotlin",
  "summary": "Kotlin map/list assignments",
  "line": "%p = %v",
  "bare": "",
  "empty_map": "mapOf<String, Any?>()"
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B base
inherits behaviour but never identity: the aliases, notes and summary of the
parent are not adopted.
.
.SS Fields
.PD 0
.TP 16
.B root
Name of the root node;
.B \-\-root
overrides it.
.TP
.B path
How root and the path chain combine.  Default
.BR %r%c .
.TP
.B key
One mapping key.  Default
.BR [%s] .
.TP
.B bare
Alternative key form used when the key is an identifier, as
.B .%r
is in JavaScript.  Empty disables it.
.TP
.B index
One sequence index.  Default
.BR [%s] .
.TP
.B join
Inserted between segments, never before the first.
.TP
.B index_base
0, or 1 for languages that count arrays from one.
.TP
.B line
The whole line.  Default
.BR "%p = %v" .
.TP
.B header
List of lines emitted once, before the first leaf.
.TP
.B quote
Quoting style for string values; see below.
.TP
.B key_quote
Quoting style for keys.  Defaults to
.BR quote .
.TP
.B quote_numbers
True to quote numbers like strings, as the
.B perl
notation does.
.TP
.B trim_floats
True to print 1.0 as 1.
.TP
.B repr_scalars
True to spell every scalar with Python's
.BR repr ().
.TP
.BR true ", " false ", " null
The three literals.
.TP
.BR empty_map ", " empty_seq
Literals for containers with nothing in them.
.TP
.B compat_quirks
True to honour
.B \-\-perl\-compat
in this notation.
.TP
.BR name ", " summary ", " aliases ", " notes
What
.B \-\-list\-templates
and
.B \-\-help\-template
show.
.PD
.
.SS Placeholders
.PD 0
.TP 6
.B %r
Root name (in
.BR path ", " header ")," 
or a key's raw, unquoted text (in
.BR key ", " bare ).
.TP
.B %c
The chain of rendered path segments (in
.BR path ).
.TP
.B %s
The segment as a literal in the target language (in
.BR key ", " index ).
.TP
.B %p
The finished path (in
.BR line ).
.TP
.B %v
The finished value (in
.BR line ).
.TP
.B %%
A literal percent sign.
.PD
.PP
Expansion runs on the template, never on the text it produced, so a value
containing
.B %v
is data rather than markup.
.
.SS Quoting styles
.PD 0
.TP 10
.B json
Double quotes with JSON escapes.  Correct for JavaScript, C++, Go, Rust, R
and jq.
.TP
.B python
Python
.BR repr ().
.TP
.B perl
Double quotes, escapes applied only under
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B shell
POSIX single quotes.
.TP
.B sql
SQL single quotes, doubled rather than escaped.
.TP
.B raw
No quoting;
.B \-e
still escapes CR, LF and TAB.
.TP
.B pointer
RFC 6901 token escaping; for keys.
.PD
.
.SS Search path
A bare
.BI \-\-template " NAME"
that is not built in is looked for as
.IB NAME .json
and then
.IB NAME .toml
in each directory of
.BR $JSON_DUMP_TEMPLATES ,
then in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/leafdump/templates .
Built\-in names always resolve first and cannot be shadowed, so a file cannot
quietly redefine
.B perl
for a script that expected it.  An argument containing a path separator, or
ending in
.B .json
or
.BR .toml ,
is read as a file.
.
.SH FORMATS
Availability is a property of a
.IR direction ,
not of a format as a whole: TOML can always be read using the standard
library's
.B tomllib
but needs
.B tomli\-w
to be written, so it may be listed as usable for input only.
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 18
.B json
JSON (RFC 8259).  Always available.
.TP
.B jsonl
Newline\-delimited JSON.  Always available.
.TP
.B repr
Python literal, read via
.BR ast.literal_eval .
Always available.
.TP
.BR perl ", " python ", " javascript ", " cpp ", " go ", " rust ", " ruby ", " php ", " lua ", " r ", " jq ", " jsonpath ", " jsonpointer ", " dotted ", " shell
Pseudocode path dumps, one per built\-in template.  Output only.  See
.B OUTPUT NOTATIONS
and
.BR TEMPLATES .
.TP
.B pseudocode
The path dump in whatever notation
.B \-\-template
named, including one read from a file.  Output only.
.TP
.B yaml
YAML 1.1, safe subset only.  Needs
.BR PyYAML .
.TP
.B json5
JSON5.  Needs
.B json5
or
.BR pyjson5 .
.TP
.B toon
Token\-Oriented Object Notation.  Needs
.BR toon\-format .
.TP
.B toml
TOML 1.0.  Reading is standard library; writing needs
.BR tomli\-w .
.TP
.B msgpack
MessagePack.  Needs
.BR msgpack .
.TP
.B cbor
CBOR (RFC 8949).  Needs
.BR cbor2 .
.TP
.B bson
BSON.  Needs
.B pymongo
(not the unrelated PyPI package named
.BR bson ).
.TP
.B avro
Apache Avro container files.  Needs
.BR fastavro .
.TP
.B nestedtext
NestedText.  Needs
.BR nestedtext .
.TP
.B protobuf\-struct
.BR google.protobuf.Value ,
the schemaless well\-known type.  Needs
.BR protobuf .
.PD
.PP
Run
.B leafdump \-\-help\-format
.I NAME
for each format's round\-trip caveats.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PD 0
.TP 6
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, unreadable input, unparseable input, or a format whose
dependencies are not installed.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.PD
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Flatten a JSON document in the original notation:
.EX
leafdump config.json
.EE
.TP
The same document as Python assignments:
.EX
leafdump \-\-to python config.json
.EE
.TP
The same document as Go, or R, or jq:
.EX
leafdump \-\-template go config.json
.EE
.TP
Render with a notation of your own:
.EX
leafdump \-\-template ./kotlin.json config.json
.EE
.TP
Reproduce the original Perl script's output exactly:
.EX
leafdump \-\-perl\-compat \-e config.json
.EE
.TP
Merge three config fragments, deduplicate every array, emit YAML:
.EX
leafdump \-\-to yaml \-\-dedup base.json site.json local.json
.EE
.TP
Merge without losing conflicting values:
.EX
leafdump \-\-merge\-strategy collect \-\-to json a.json b.json
.EE
.TP
Keep each file under its own key instead of merging:
.EX
leafdump \-\-wrap\-key stem \-\-to json */settings.json
.EE
.TP
Dump a packet capture newest packet first:
.EX
leafdump \-r packets.jsonl
.EE
.TP
Convert MessagePack from a pipe to indented JSON:
.EX
producer | leafdump \-\-from msgpack \-\-to json
.EE
.TP
Find every leaf whose path mentions a preset name:
.EX
leafdump presets.json | grep '{name}'
.EE
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Width used to wrap
.B \-\-help
output.
.TP
.B JSON_DUMP_TEMPLATES
Directories searched for pseudocode templates, separated by the platform's
path separator, ahead of the XDG config directory.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the per\-user config directory searched for templates.  Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/leafdump/templates/
Pseudocode templates available as
.BI \-\-template " NAME"
without a path.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
.BR yq (1),
.BR gron (1)
.PP
.BR jq (1)
is the tool for queries, joins and transformations, at the price of a filter
language to learn;
.B leafdump
is for the other half of the work \(em finding where a value lives, reading the
shape of an unfamiliar document, or grepping a directory of them with the tools
already to hand.
.BR gron (1)
takes the same approach with a single fixed notation; the
.B javascript
template with
.B \-\-root json
is that notation.
.PP
The project roadmap describes planned work: SQL\-style filtering with embedded
pseudo\-python expressions, an interactive query shell, an out\-of\-core storage
backend for structures larger than RAM, Ibis integration, and direct
publication to document databases.
.
